// Practice quiz: Secrets from the environment
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/secrets-from-the-environment

::o1 relaxed binding::Which environment variable binds spring.datasource.username? {
=SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME#Correct. Uppercase with dots replaced by underscores.
~spring.datasource.username#Not quite. Most shells do not allow dots in variable names.
~DATASOURCE_USER#Not quite. Relaxed binding keeps the whole key.
~SPRING-DATASOURCE-USERNAME#Not quite. Dashes do not replace dots here.
}

::o2 fail fast::Why use a placeholder with no default for a required secret? {
=A missing value stops startup immediately#Correct. The failure is visible before any request is served.
~It encrypts the value#Not quite. Placeholders do not encrypt.
~It lets the app start with a blank password#Not quite. That is what a default would allow.
~It stores the secret in the jar#Not quite. It keeps the secret out of the jar.
}

::o3 rotation::A secret was pushed to a shared repository and then removed in the next commit. What must you do? {
=Rotate it#Correct. Treat it as compromised, because history and clones keep it.
~Nothing, since the latest commit is clean#Not quite. History still holds it.
~Rename the variable#Not quite. The old value still works.
~Add the file to .gitignore#Not quite. That only stops future commits.
}

::o3 logs::Why are logs a poor place for secret values? {
=Logs are copied, shipped and retained#Correct. They reach many systems and people.
~Logs slow down the app too much#Not quite. The concern is exposure.
~Secrets are too long to log#Not quite. Length is not the issue.
~Logs only work in development#Not quite. Logs run in production too.
}

::o3 error bodies::A database connection fails during a request. What should the API return to the client? {
=A generic error, with details only in server logs#Correct. Clients must not receive internal details or credentials.
~The full JDBC URL including the password#Not quite. That exposes secrets.
~The stack trace#Not quite. That exposes internals.
~The value of every environment variable#Not quite. That is a serious exposure.
}

